# workers/etl/.env.example — pfin_back_etl container surface.
#
# Per-surface secret confinement (Phase 5 Step 8; Sec joint-review surface).
# Supersedes the prior sample.env (reconciled into this .env.example convention;
# .gitignore tracks `*.env.example` via the `!.env.example` negation).
#
# Anchors (verified verbatim 2026-06-28):
#   - ARCH §5 → pfin_back_etl container env-var scope: "Postgres conn-string
#     (service_role tier; consumed only via TenantBoundConnection class);
#     PLAID_CLIENT_ID / PLAID_SECRET; FMP_API_KEY (BLS framed "free/open" in ARCH §5)."
#     ⚠ "service_role tier" is a WRITE-ROLE statement, NOT a login-identity statement.
#     `service_role` is `rolcanlogin = f` (MEASURED) — it can NEVER be the connection
#     user. See the DB LOGIN ROLE block below; ARCH §5's phrasing is queued for the
#     same correction (SELF-214 Sec joint-review v3 B1(a)).
#   - ADR-011 Lock 13 mod #3 — TenantBoundConnection: ALL pfin DB access binds
#     users_id at construction; raw psycopg connections are a fence-tbc violation.
#   - ADR-002 §6.0 — secrets discipline (Coolify-injected in production).
#   - workers/etl/src/pfin_back_etl/utils.py load_env_variables() — the names
#     the running code actually consumes (env_prefix "PFIN_" for DB params).
#
# Secret-store assignment: ALL values below are PRODUCTION-ONLY (Coolify-injected
# on cax21). See secrets-manifest.yml. Any CI/test analogue MUST take a distinct
# name (e.g. *_SANDBOX) and live ONLY in the manifest's ci_only set.
#
# REPRESENTATION NOTE (flagged for Sec joint-review): ARCH §5 phrases the DB
# secret as a single "Postgres conn-string". The incumbent code consumes DISCRETE
# PFIN_DB_* params (utils.py). This file mirrors the running code (discrete) to
# avoid breaking production; the discrete-vs-conn-string shape is a representation
# difference, not a scope difference.
#
# TBC NOTE: the DB connection MUST be consumed via TenantBoundConnection per Lock 13
# mod #3. The TBC class is now implemented (connection.py) and is the single
# sanctioned engine factory; fence coverage (scripts/ci/fence-tbc-pfin-back-etl.sh)
# is DevOps-owned. See workers/CLAUDE.md ("ETL CI coverage is a tracked follow-up").
#
# ============================================================================
# DB LOGIN ROLE = `pfin_etl`   (SELF-214 B8 — F/CTO-ratified option (B),
# 2026-08-02: a DEDICATED login role, NOT shared `authenticator`)
# ============================================================================
# The ETL connects AS `pfin_etl`, a dedicated NOINHERIT LOGIN role created by an
# Architect migration with membership in `service_role` + `authenticated`.
# It is NOT `service_role`, NOT `postgres`, and NOT `authenticator`.
# Measured facts behind this:
#   · `service_role` : rolcanlogin = f → transport-impossible as a login identity.
#                      Any doc saying a worker "connects as service_role" names a
#                      role that cannot be connected as.
#   · `pfin_etl`     : rolcanlogin = t, rolinherit = f (NOINHERIT), NOT BYPASSRLS,
#                      NOT the `pfin` owner; member of `service_role`+`authenticated`.
#
# Privileged writes still execute AS `service_role`, assumed per-transaction via
# `SET LOCAL ROLE service_role` inside the worker (ADR-023 write role-of-record is
# UNCHANGED — only the connection/login role is pinned). Reads that must resolve
# `auth.uid()` assume `authenticated` via TenantBoundConnection.impersonate().
# The worker code is IDENTICAL under `pfin_etl` and `authenticator` — the two
# memberships are the same, so this was a NAME change, not a model change.
#
# WHY A DEDICATED ROLE (B8 option (B)) RATHER THAN SHARED `authenticator`.
# `secrets-manifest.yml:61` states the ETL "uses its own login credential",
# distinguished from provider-sync's. Sharing `authenticator` would have made that
# line false and widened ADR-023 condition C1's rotation coupling from two
# consumers to three. With `pfin_etl` the manifest is accurate AS WRITTEN and
# **C1 does NOT extend to the ETL** — C1 stays PostgREST + provider-sync, and this
# worker's credential rotates INDEPENDENTLY of both. That is the whole point.
#
# WHY NOT `postgres`. The prior value here was `<example::postgres.your_project_ref>`
# — the Supabase-pooler username form for the schema OWNER. Pasting the Supabase
# connection string as shown yields the owner identity, and the owner INVERTS every
# fence migration 054 relies on: table grants become decorative (ownership already
# carries INSERT+UPDATE+DELETE+TRUNCATE), and the append-only triggers become
# bypassable by the writer itself (`ALTER TABLE … DISABLE TRIGGER`,
# `session_replication_role = 'replica'` — both REPRODUCED in a rolled-back txn).
# Under `pfin_etl`→`service_role` the writer is neither owner nor superuser, so it
# can do neither, and 054's `grant insert … to service_role` becomes load-bearing
# exactly as its header claims. Concrete value below, NOT a placeholder, so the
# template cannot steer a deploy back to the owner identity.
#
# 🔑 DEPLOY HANDOFF — "role exists" ≠ "ETL can connect".
# Migration `055` creates `pfin_etl` **NOLOGIN, with NO password** (a credential must
# never sit in the repo — and a migration file is committed, diffed and mirrored to
# GitHub). The role ships INERT and is switched on at deploy, ONCE, by an operator —
# never from a committed file. TWO statements, and THE ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING:
#
#     \password pfin_etl          -- psql meta-command: prompts, computes the verifier
#                                 -- CLIENT-SIDE. Role is still NOLOGIN → inert.
#     ALTER ROLE pfin_etl LOGIN;  -- carries NO secret; safe in history and logs.
#
# ⚠ STEP 2 RUN WITHOUT STEP 1 (`LOGIN` set, password never set) — THE ONE DANGEROUS
# ORDERING. It succeeds SILENTLY and leaves exactly the LOGIN-with-no-password state
# `055` is shaped to prevent, which is reachable under a `trust` line. In the correct
# order `\password` sets only the password, so the credential lands while the role is
# still NOLOGIN and the role becomes reachable only once a password exists.
#   (This is the ONLY place a `pg_hba` caveat belongs. NOLOGIN-first removes the
#   dependency entirely — `rolcanlogin` is checked from the role attribute BEFORE any
#   auth method is consulted — so the `trust` exposure exists only in this mis-ordered
#   failure state. A general caveat elsewhere would re-imply a dependency the design
#   eliminated.)
#   Backstop: re-applying `055` raises a WARNING on a pre-existing LOGIN-with-no-
#   password role, so it catches this mis-ordering too — do not strip that guard
#   thinking it only covers manual provisioning.
# Operator must be superuser, or hold CREATEROLE / ADMIN OPTION on the role — a
# deployer lacking it gets a permission error at step 1 and may reach for the
# PROHIBITED single statement as a workaround, which is the worst outcome.
#
# ⚠ BE PRECISE ABOUT WHAT `\password` BUYS — DO NOT WRITE "THE SECRET ISN'T LOGGED".
# It hashes client-side and then SENDS `ALTER USER … PASSWORD 'SCRAM-SHA-256$…'`.
# That is DDL, so wherever statement logging is on, **the verifier IS still written to
# the server log.** What changes is WHAT is logged, not whether:
#   · PLAINTEXT NEVER LEAVES THE CLIENT — not the wire, not the log, not
#     `~/.psql_history` (which the earlier "use interactive psql" mitigation missed).
#   · THE LOGGED VERIFIER IS NOT A USABLE CREDENTIAL — StoredKey + ServerKey only; a
#     client proof needs ClientKey, not derivable from StoredKey.
#   · RESIDUAL = an OFFLINE attack against that verifier, bounded by THE SECRET'S
#     ENTROPY and the iteration count. Generate it — `openssl rand -hex 32` (256-bit,
#     mirroring WORKER_ADMISSION_SHARED_SECRET). This is not decoration: the generated
#     high-entropy value is PRECISELY what makes that residual acceptable, and a
#     human-chosen password would not. The generator and the risk statement are
#     load-bearing on each other — carry both or neither.
#   · Categorically better than cleartext, which is replayable immediately at zero
#     cost — but it is NOT "nothing is logged."
#
# ⚠ LOGGING POSTURE — MEASUREMENT SCOPE. `log_statement = ddl` was measured on the
# LOCAL stack. **The V1 production stack does not exist yet and has not been measured.**
# Treat statement logging as ENABLED on any target until verified otherwise THERE —
# assuming it off is the failure-open direction. And `log_statement` is not the only
# knob: `log_min_duration_statement` can log a statement in full independently. The
# pair that actually settles it for a target:
#     show log_statement;  show log_min_duration_statement;
#
# The single atomic `ALTER ROLE … WITH LOGIN PASSWORD '<plaintext>'` is PROHIBITED
# (Sec joint-review B10): wherever statement logging is on it writes the ETL's
# credential to the server log in cleartext, and Postgres does not redact passwords
# from the log. The prohibition does not depend on any per-stack measurement.
#
# Rotation    = `\password pfin_etl` + restart the ETL container ONLY (no coordinated
#               PostgREST/provider-sync redeploy — the ADR-023 C1 coupling this role
#               escapes). Revocation = `ALTER ROLE pfin_etl NOLOGIN`.
# Until step 2 runs the container CANNOT connect — fail-closed by design, but a
# REQUIRED deploy step, not an automatic one.
#
# `055`'s header is CANONICAL for this handoff and carries the full reasoning
# (SCRAM key derivation, the measured `password_encryption` / iteration count, why the
# two-step preserves the NOLOGIN property). This block is the operational summary a
# deployer needs at the point of use — if the two ever disagree, `055` wins and this
# is the copy to fix.
#
# ⚠ NOINHERIT CONSEQUENCE — READ BEFORE ADDING A NEW ETL WRITE PATH. Under
# `pfin_etl` the session holds NO privileges until an explicit `SET [LOCAL] ROLE`.
# Any pfin statement issued without one fails 42501. nav_daily.py does this at both
# its enumeration and its checkpoint-INSERT; new write paths must too. This bites
# `impersonate()` in particular — its `reset role` returns the session to the
# privilege-less login role, so the CALLER must assume the write role before writing.
#
# (SEPARATE, and NOT a blocker on this role decision: core.py's global-reference path
# targets incumbent cax21 tables that NO V1 migration creates — it is dead against V1
# under any credential and fails loud with UndefinedTable. Not a grants problem, not a
# credential problem; it is an open V1-scope question owned by PM + Architect. See
# workers/CLAUDE.md. The V1 ETL write surface is pfin.asset / eod_price / cpi_u_index.)

# --- pfin Postgres transport (login role = pfin_etl; writes AS service_role per ADR-023) ---
# HOST / PORT / NAME mirror workers/provider-sync/.env.example verbatim — same
# database, one coherent contract; DevOps-pinned at deploy.
#
# ⚠ PROHIBITED VARIABLE — `PGTZ` MUST NEVER BE SET FOR THIS CONTAINER.
#   This container connects via psycopg, i.e. libpq, and MEASURED 2026-08-04: libpq sends
#   PGTZ as a startup parameter that OVERRIDES the database-level TimeZone pin
#   (pg_settings.source becomes `client`). It would move THIS container's session zone
#   alone, so the deployment would be half-pinned and the production database would still
#   read UTC to anyone inspecting it. See deployment-runbook §4.1 + §10 TZ-1, which asserts
#   the read-back per-container for exactly this reason.
#   `TZ` is NOT the same thing and does NOT affect the DB session — setting TZ=UTC is a
#   no-op here and must not be mistaken for the pin.
PFIN_DB_USER=pfin_etl
PFIN_DB_HOST=localhost
PFIN_DB_PORT=5432
PFIN_DB_NAME=pfin
PFIN_DB_PASSWORD=          # secret (production_only) — the `pfin_etl` password. NOT authenticator's, NOT PostgREST's: no C1 coupling, rotates independently. Coolify-injected; the SAME value must be applied to the role at deploy via `\password` — NOT a plaintext ALTER ROLE (see DEPLOY HANDOFF).

# OPTIONAL — NOT a secret. libpq sslmode for the ETL's database connection.
#   UNSET / BLANK -> "require". Leave it unset in production: the DEFAULT is the
#   security-load-bearing part, and an environment that forgets this still demands TLS.
#   Set "disable" ONLY for the local Supabase CLI stack, which offers no TLS — without
#   it the ETL cannot connect to the local stack at all (BACKLOG §7.6 S11).
#   An unrecognised value RAISES rather than falling back, so a typo cannot silently
#   change the transport posture. Hardening values (verify-ca, verify-full) accepted.
PFIN_DB_SSLMODE=

# FMP (Financial Modeling Prep) — security-price reference-data ingestion.
# Required by code (utils.py raises if absent).
FMP_API_KEY=<financial-modeling-prep-api-key>

# BLS (Bureau of Labor Statistics) — CPI-U reference-data ingestion.
# DISCREPANCY (flagged for Sec/ARCH, NOT silently reconciled): ARCH §5 frames BLS
# as "free/open" (no key), but the incumbent code
# (utils.py) REQUIRES BLS_API_KEY (raises ValueError if absent). The BLS public
# API accepts an optional registration key that raises rate limits. Retained
# here to match the running production reality; flagged so ARCH §5 and the code
# requirement can be reconciled deliberately.
BLS_API_KEY=<bureau-of-labor-statistics-api-key>
BLS_SERIES_ID=CUUR0000SA0

# Plaid credentials — scheduled-poll worker (ARCH §5 lists Plaid creds on this
# container). NOT yet consumed by the incumbent ETL code; they ACTIVATE with the
# Wave 6 Plaid scheduled-poll worker (Backend). Listed here per ARCH §5 so the
# container's secret contract is complete; sandbox tier in V1.0 per workers/CLAUDE.md.
PLAID_CLIENT_ID=<plaid-client-id>
PLAID_SECRET=<plaid-secret>
